Date:     Wed, 10 Nov 93 5:00:04 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject:  Interstate Myths

>From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
>Subject: (fwd) Clipper and Tipper on Route 666
>
>And unlike the National Interstate Highway System, to which the NII is
>often compared (perhaps wrongly...), there is absolutely no need for
>the government to get involved. No land is needed (as was the case in
>the 1950s when the Interstates were being built), and no centralized
>planning is needed.

The exerpt above from Tim May's otherwise excellent attack on the
data taxway proposal illustrates a background assumption that is
false.  There was never any need for the government to get involved
with building a national taxway system, either.  Rhetorically, this
information may not be persuasive to many, but it's good to know,
especially for libertarians.

The Interstate system was originally a fairly direct subsidy to the
car industry.  Policy-makers thought the industry a public good
(this was during the 1920s), and wanted to support it.  In the
process, they harmed the railroads which provided more economically
and ecologically sound transportation between major cities.

The New Deal of Emperor Roosevelt II lent two new rationales to
the construction of the Interstate system: jobs, and war preparedness.
Cutting unemployment was a major concern of economic policy for the
first time in US history (this was the first time in US history
that there was that much unemployment to worry about).  Putting
unemployed construction union workers (back when unions still could
legally exclude women and minorities, and did with a vengeance) to
work building roads, all financed by higher taxes, debt, and inflation,
"solved" this political problem.  Then, as the "Depression" (before
then, "panic" was the term for the bust end of the business cycle; it
was deemed to upsetting, so "depression" was substituted, which is now
deemed so upsetting that "recession" is preferred) continued with no
end in sight, FDR switched to a strategy of getting the US into a
foreign war, successfully (we now call that war WWII).  Along these
lines, the Interstate system was built in order to facilitate troop
transport as well as other military supplies nationally.

Thus, by the time it was finished by the Eisenhower administration in
the 1950s (Ike liked Hitler's Autobahn), that's what we had.  A
subsidy for Big Auto makers in Detroit (who were already falling behind
foreign competition due to this sort of intervention) built with
Big Labor pork contracts to military specifications, all at taxpayer
expense.

Furthermore, its failures are now readily apparent.  Americans make fun
of Russians standing in bread lines while they drive two to five hours a
day in traffic jams, polluting the air with smelly exhaust, rubbernecking
at accidents (more Americans die each year from traffic accidents than
did in the Vietnam war, and the Feds still have sovereign immunity from
liability for design defects)...  The Feds use theftway maintenance and
construction funds to bribe States into depriving adults of their rights
to drink (21 or over only, thank you!), to ride a motorcycle without a
helmet, to ride in a car without a seatbelt (both of which arguably
reduce safety through low visibility and encouragement of more reckless
driving because the costs to the driver are lower), to drive over 55 mph
in most places, 65 in a few places, on roads that were designed to
handle an average traffic speed of 70 mph...

I may or may not be worthwhile to go into all this, but there's no reason
at the very least to give the statists bastards any ground they haven't
already stolen.

