From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: ECON: Good Morons and Bad Theories.

>So, per capita GNP in the U.S. is only $22,500? I find this incredibly hard
>to believe. It seems about an order of magnitude off. The net revenue
>per employee of Sun Microsystems was $325,000 in the last fiscal year, 
>and this completely ignores any additional productivity I may have
>outside of my job at Sun. 
> 
>I could believe $200,000, I could even believe $100,000, without seeing 
>actual numbers, but I don't believe $22,500. Are the Marching Morons truly
>here already?
> 
>  Rowan
 
  I'll join the Marching Morons this time.
I don't know what you mean by 'net revenue' here, but usually revenue
means the gross income; if you resell parts you buy from your suppliers, 
they are counted in revenue, but still do not represent *your* product.
In a chain of suppliers, original products may be counted many times.
GDP/GNP calculations eliminate such multiple accounting and thus produce
figures several times as small as a simple sum of the outputs (which has
no economic meaning whatsoever, being a mixed indicator of the maturity
of the economy, and the degree of its vertical integration).
   Also, if your company is in a capital-intensive business (which it is),
its revenue per employee is higher than it is, say, for a freelance
janitor.   
   Besides, it helps if your company employs expensive labor, and does
well in general.
 
   All these factors make Sun's revenue per employee much, much bigger
than GNP per capita.  The latter is actually not that far from average
personal income (+ maybe %30 for industrial consumption and investment),
which is definitely not $200,000.
 
                   ---   ---   ---
 
   I lost my respect to the economic calculations, however, because all
existing theories are completely unable to assess the value of:
 
  - qualitatively new products,   which are the essence of the current
                                  stage of progress;
 
  - natural resources of the planet and the rest of the Universe,
    which represent most of the real capital we have available;
 
  - self-service and non-commercial personal interactions,
    which contribute most of the value to each of us;
 
  - progress of science, arts, languages, social structures,
    shifts in belief systems, and lots of other areas where
    a reduction to a single quantifiable factor ('$') is inadequate.
 
 
  After all of that, I would compare calculations of economic figures
to observations of my body fat ratio;  while representing important
indicators and reflecting the health of a low-level functional layer
of the system, these figures are hardly relevant for understanding
the evolution of the system as a whole.
 
