Date: Tue, 9 Nov 1993 23:19:18 -0800
From: jamie@netcom.com (Jamie Dinkelacker)
Subject: Physics as Therapy

wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore) writes in #93-11-206>

>i am trying to model my own education on the accumulation of knowledge 
>in the physical sciences. it is disgusting to me to invest all my energy 
>in an endeavor that is mud, sludge, assumptions piled on opinions 
>touching on facts. it is infuriating that almost no one in 
>education/psychology takes time to think through to the fundamental 
>implications of an idea. without this kind of thought, researchers are 
>continually re-assuming, cross-inventing, undoing one another's thought. 

I suspect there's more to this issue than just this. 

The social/behavioral disciplines are plagued by a fundamental problem:
absence of measurement rigor. Without this basis, it's nearly impossible to
have agreement on premises, let alone be rigorous in derivations.

These disciplines lack conventional standards that (1) identify key
variables (e.g., no STP); (2) establish conventional means for ascribing
symbols to phenomena (e.g., no consistent measurement instruments -- Likert
scales or semantic differentials, fractionalization scales, ... just don't
count); or (3) establish units of measure (e.g., no meter, gram, liter,
coulomb, joule, ...).

In these disciplines it's as if using someone else's terminology is like
using their toothbrush. Everybody has their own, which inhibits the
accretion of knowledge. And the promotion and reward structure in academia
is to typically generate more and more publications, almost independent of
their quality. (Ph.D. = Piled Higher & Deeper.) I remember one prof at
Michigan State during the 70s whose CB handle was "Big Vita." Many academic
departments are white-collar welfare supported by your friendly local tax
collector.

Worst yet, many of the journals -- especially in communication -- don't
publish negative results, so reinventing the square wheel keeps recurring.
The thrill of the day is often "replication with modification" not just
straight replication. So who's to know what works and, just as importantly,
what doesn't?

Yet, some very, very brilliant minds are from these fields, and they have
stunning insights about human behavior and society: Durkheim, Mead,
Sullivan, Cooley, Rodgers, Zimbardo, Milgram, Campbell, Lewin, Festinger,
(for a quick 10) ... are just a few. This is an exciting area of study, but
it's best to think of these disciplines as nascent, inchoate rather than
fully formed and on their stride. 

Humans are tricky things to study. What is humor? Perversity? Adoration?
Love...? But, just because the inquiry is complex, frustrating and at times
bewildering, doesn't make it not worthwhile. Especially if your central
interest is human behavior and its manifestations (e.g. organizational
development, interpersonal relations, cross cultural communication ... ).
Plus, if this is where a person's core interests rest, here's where the
main game is played.

Admittedly, progress is glacially slow; but neither is it stopped nor
retrograde. (BTW: my doctorate is in communication research.)

